Project: PPI Tracker

Goal:
Prevent two users from editing the same tracker data at the same time.

Important:
- Do not overwrite or remove existing user changes.
- Do not create or apply MySQL tables until confirming whether the supervisor has already created them.
- This workspace currently contains the PPI Tracker desktop client. The backend repository is not available here, so backend API and database work requires the backend project.
- Keep the existing Edit in Cell and Edit Entity user flows.

Current desktop code locations:
- src/ppiTracker/mainWindow.py
  - onDoubleClick(): opens the full Edit Entity dialog.
  - beginDirectCellEdit(): starts direct Edit in Cell.
  - saveDirectCellEdit(): saves a direct cell edit.
  - showCellContextMenu(): contains the right-click Edit in Cell action.
- src/ppiTracker/entityEditorDialog.py
  - EntityEditorDialog.save(): saves full-entity and multi-row edits.
- src/ppiTracker/apiClient.py
  - updateEntity(): sends entity updates to the backend.

Final required behavior:

1. Normal click
- Clicking a cell or row only selects it.
- Do not create a lock.

2. Edit in Cell
- Right-click -> Edit in Cell.
- Before opening the editor, acquire a lock only for that cell.
- Example: anitaHero / Status.
- If another user owns the lock, do not open the editor.
- Show: “Status for anitaHero is currently being edited by User A.”
- Release the lock on Save, Cancel, Escape, or editor close.

3. Full Edit Entity dialog
- Before opening the full dialog for one row, acquire an entity/row lock.
- An entity lock prevents another user from opening the full dialog for the same row.
- It also prevents direct cell editing inside that row while the full dialog is open.
- Release the lock on Save, Cancel, or close.

4. Multiple selected rows
- When opening Edit Entity for multiple selected rows, lock all selected rows as one batch.
- This must be all-or-nothing.
- If every selected row is available, lock all and open the dialog.
- If one row is locked, lock none and do not open the dialog.
- Show the locked entity and lock owner.

5. Lock hierarchy
- An entity lock blocks every cell lock for that entity.
- A cell lock blocks another lock for the same cell.
- A cell lock for Status does not block a cell lock for Notes in the same entity.
- A cell lock blocks an entity lock for the same entity.

6. Lock expiry
- Locks are temporary.
- Lock duration: 2 to 5 minutes.
- Renew active locks every 30 to 60 seconds while the editor is open.
- If the app crashes, expired locks become available automatically.
- If renewal fails, preserve the user draft and disable Save until the state is refreshed.

7. Revision check on every save
- Store and return a revision number for every editable cell.
- The client saves with the revision that existed when editing began.
- The backend accepts the save only if that revision still matches.
- If data changed while editing, return HTTP 409 Conflict and do not overwrite newer data.
- Locks prevent normal collisions; revisions protect against expired locks, imports, scripts, rollbacks, and other update paths.

MySQL lock-table requirements:

Table name:
tracker_edit_locks

Required columns:
- id
- project_id
- entity_id
- lock_scope VARCHAR(32) NOT NULL
- field_key VARCHAR(255) NOT NULL
- studio VARCHAR(255) NOT NULL
- user_id VARCHAR(255) NOT NULL
- lock_token CHAR(64) NOT NULL
- created_at
- updated_at
- expires_at

Rules:
- lock_scope currently supports: entity and cell.
- Use VARCHAR(32), not ENUM, to support future scopes.
- Backend must validate allowed lock_scope values.
- field_key is * for an entity lock.
- field_key is the real field name, such as status, for a cell lock.
- studio distinguishes users with the same username.
- The user and studio must not be part of the lock-target unique rule.
- Only one user may own the same target at one time.
- Backend transaction logic must enforce entity-versus-cell lock conflicts.

Suggested lock API:

POST /api/tracker/locks/acquire
PATCH /api/tracker/locks/{lock_token}
DELETE /api/tracker/locks/{lock_token}

Cell-lock request:
{
  "scope": "cell",
  "project_id": "project-name",
  "entity_id": "entity-id",
  "field_key": "status"
}

Entity-lock batch request:
{
  "scope": "entity",
  "project_id": "project-name",
  "entity_ids": [
    "anitaHero-id",
    "bipedHero-id"
  ]
}

Successful response:
{
  "lock_token": "secure-server-token",
  "expires_at": "UTC date and time"
}

Conflict response:
HTTP 409 Conflict

{
  "error": "locked",
  "conflicts": [
    {
      "entity_id": "anitaHero-id",
      "field_key": "status",
      "locked_by": "User A",
      "studio": "studio-name"
    }
  ]
}

Save request changes:
Extend updateEntity() so saves include:
- changed values
- expected cell revisions
- lock token

Multi-row saves:
- Add an atomic batch-update backend endpoint.
- Save all selected rows together, or save none if any lock or revision conflict exists.
- Do not keep the current behavior where some rows can save before a later row fails.

